WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.560
How can a single gray-green patch on a tree show different kinds of symbiosis?

2
00:00:05.940 --> 00:00:08.640
Start with the patch, then look closely inside it.

3
00:00:11.950 --> 00:00:13.530
This patch is a lichen.

4
00:00:14.790 --> 00:00:19.750
Its main partners are a fungus and an alga, or sometimes a cyanobacterium.

5
00:00:20.870 --> 00:00:24.130
They remain different species while sharing a common body.

6
00:00:25.390 --> 00:00:28.430
That long-lasting, close relationship is symbiosis.

7
00:00:29.583 --> 00:00:35.943
Watch what moves. The photosynthetic partner makes sugar, which feeds both partners.

8
00:00:37.423 --> 00:00:40.463
Fungus threads shelter those cells and help keep them moist.

9
00:00:41.803 --> 00:00:43.803
Rainwater spreads through the lichen body.

10
00:00:45.143 --> 00:00:46.423
Each partner gains something.

11
00:00:47.483 --> 00:00:49.063
Close contact matters.

12
00:00:50.303 --> 00:00:52.143
A bird may eat a seed and fly away.

13
00:00:53.443 --> 00:00:56.323
That brief meeting is not symbiosis with the plant.

14
00:00:57.783 --> 00:01:00.943
Lichen partners remain together, often for a long time.

15
00:01:01.933 --> 00:01:05.813
When all partners benefit, the relationship is mutualism.

16
00:01:07.073 --> 00:01:08.133
Now zoom back out.

17
00:01:09.393 --> 00:01:11.193
The whole lichen sits on tree bark.

18
00:01:12.473 --> 00:01:14.153
The lichen gains a place to live.

19
00:01:15.353 --> 00:01:16.693
What does the tree gain or lose?

20
00:01:22.750 --> 00:01:26.250
For ordinary lichens on bark, the tree seems unaffected.

21
00:01:27.990 --> 00:01:30.650
Experts say these lichens almost never harm the tree.

22
00:01:31.990 --> 00:01:36.030
When a partner benefits and the other is unaffected, the relationship is

23
00:01:36.030 --> 00:01:37.030
commensalism.

24
00:01:37.883 --> 00:01:41.323
Sort symbiosis by what each partner gains or loses.

25
00:01:42.683 --> 00:01:46.083
That rule separates the relationships hiding in the same patch.

26
00:01:47.303 --> 00:01:52.143
It also handles harmful partnerships, as long as the partners live together closely.

27
00:01:53.417 --> 00:01:57.017
A blacklegged tick can stay on a deer and drink blood for days.

28
00:01:58.397 --> 00:01:59.397
The tick gains food.

29
00:02:00.617 --> 00:02:03.377
The deer loses blood and may get irritated skin.

30
00:02:04.677 --> 00:02:06.977
This helped-harmed pattern is parasitism.

31
00:02:08.067 --> 00:02:10.607
A parasite is not the same as a predator.

32
00:02:11.887 --> 00:02:14.747
A predator usually kills prey during a brief attack.

33
00:02:16.007 --> 00:02:20.067
A parasite usually feeds for days or longer without killing its host.

34
00:02:21.150 --> 00:02:24.290
None of these partners chooses kindness or harm.

35
00:02:25.630 --> 00:02:29.730
Ways of living can continue when they support survival and reproduction.

36
00:02:31.310 --> 00:02:33.570
Mutualism can support both partners.

37
00:02:35.030 --> 00:02:38.170
Parasitism can support the parasite while harming its host.

38
00:02:39.250 --> 00:02:42.330
The names describe outcomes, not personalities.

39
00:02:43.850 --> 00:02:45.950
Mutualism is the helped-helped pattern.

40
00:02:47.370 --> 00:02:49.050
Commensalism is helped-unaffected.

41
00:02:50.750 --> 00:02:52.110
Parasitism is helped-harmed.

42
00:02:53.430 --> 00:02:56.110
Other outcome pairs do not match these taught kinds.

43
00:02:57.233 --> 00:02:59.673
So begin with partners, not labels.

44
00:03:01.133 --> 00:03:04.593
Follow what each receives, what each gives, and whether either is harmed.

45
00:03:05.873 --> 00:03:08.593
Then the name follows from the outcomes you can support.
